#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124255 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "37734955-23b7-52d1-881d-0c77f672c35a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3162" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2.$ Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(3, -1, 1)= -2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{x}(3,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz+2y\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{y}(3,-1,1)=1 \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{z}(3,-1,1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(3,-1,1) & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-1,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2-(x-3)+(y+1)-3(z-1) \\\\  & =-2-x+3+y+1-3z+3 \\\\  & =5-x+y-3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "839eb8fd-39b8-5f1f-b833-7134d39cdf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8674" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1)(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =(1)^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.1,0.9) & ≈1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.1-1,0.9-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.2-0.1 \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "54ac5be6-5b6a-58a3-a960-0d9f18e7fc88" :question/math-academy-id "e-8692" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,5)=2, \\quad  f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad  f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,5)+∇f(1,2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,5),{f}_{y}(1,2,5),{f}_{z}(1,2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01,$ $y=2.02,$ and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,2.02,4.95) & ≈2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,2.02-2,4.95-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =2+0.04+0.02+0.1 \\\\  & =2.16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632fe9ac-23ea-537f-8867-fd2753f28891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263857} {:answer/id #uuid "9b914279-ba9d-5a03-95e5-b51aa67373f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264330} {:answer/id #uuid "a25307ac-a75d-5dc5-be60-125050a4cf4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264377} {:answer/id #uuid "ac62ce9c-a6e3-5bb3-9853-9211cd95db40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264483} {:answer/id #uuid "ae67de65-0019-5fa8-9dae-30b168510beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae67de65-0019-5fa8-9dae-30b168510beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264500} :answer-field/id #uuid "d586335b-ba1b-50a7-be59-ad42f8b80319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4947c49-4be9-5752-b327-3eab9b3c6101" :question/math-academy-id "q-129632" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=-6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-0.99).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-0.99) & ≈-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3-6(0.01)+3(0.01) \\\\  & =-3-0.06+0.03 \\\\  & =-3.03\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197482a9-25ae-57d8-afdf-2fcd053e99ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263241} {:answer/id #uuid "4aed0798-8f9f-58ff-8eed-d4db0291f98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263644} {:answer/id #uuid "92eea536-3ea6-59db-83e9-6446c1bf9b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264258} {:answer/id #uuid "aef9680f-3937-5e10-813f-e8f4c13df817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264507} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f77c51-c9bf-5c2e-ba90-a5748a9b68bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef9680f-3937-5e10-813f-e8f4c13df817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264507} :answer-field/id #uuid "937e48d9-f711-5f48-9a36-246c8add4acb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed8e93d2-0997-5503-b2f5-fb92ac71a49d" :question/math-academy-id "q-129655" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,0)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(-1,1,0)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(-1,1,0)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ estimate the value of $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-1,1,0)+∇f(-1,1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-1,z-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(-1,1,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,1,0),{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1,$ $y=1.01,$ and $z=-0.01$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,1.01,-0.01) & ≈-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1+1,1.01-1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0.01,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+1(0)-2(0.01)+0(-0.01) \\\\  & =-1+0-0.02+0 \\\\  & =-1.02\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df30f23-b0a0-54f4-9a1e-d7b1a1fd4867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383141} {:answer/id #uuid "373df19d-57e9-547a-ba9b-9963fd440fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383142} {:answer/id #uuid "ab944cbb-f62f-591c-a4e0-6083721c8f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383143} {:answer/id #uuid "ca13d030-e125-56a6-bbe5-61d11556f593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383144} {:answer/id #uuid "9f83c54d-5936-513c-9cb6-026a3dbcbec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab944cbb-f62f-591c-a4e0-6083721c8f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383143} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2df20ee-da83-5337-b5c6-066773381479" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b880160-8e82-545a-ac66-2d0d38f21ec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129659" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=12,\\,{f}_{x}(3,2,1)=4,\\,{f}_{y}(3,2,1)=12,\\,{f}_{z}(3,2,1)=36.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2,1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.97,2.1,1.04).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,2,1)+∇f(3,2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =12+⟨{f}_{x}(3,2,1),{f}_{y}(3,2,1),{f}_{z}(3,2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =12+⟨4,12,36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.97,2.1,1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.97,$ $y=2.1,$ and $z=1.04$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.97,2.1,1.04) & ≈12+⟨4,12,36⟩\\cdot ⟨2.97-3,2.1-2,1.04-1⟩ \\\\  & =12+⟨4,12,36⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.03,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =12+4(-0.03)+12(0.1)+36(0.04) \\\\  & =12-0.12+1.2+1.44 \\\\  & =14.52\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c81a8f-8b3c-59a9-b3bc-1e3c6f1d0f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383148} {:answer/id #uuid "e7211b05-cdef-52df-b9f5-794f91721ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-1+x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383149} {:answer/id #uuid "05323a66-a7b1-5534-8840-c3fe203d1155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383150} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57c79-235f-5c6f-bbb8-7e965feef750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-3+x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383151} {:answer/id #uuid "6a76e60c-a626-557c-88ff-af8b91dc41a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2-x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a76e60c-a626-557c-88ff-af8b91dc41a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2-x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383152} :answer-field/id #uuid "f102ebcb-0a48-5085-94bc-c1ac0bda4076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "842a0b51-a8a6-5106-93ae-c9c478945bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129742" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=-x{y}^{2}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1)=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-{y}^{2}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-2xy &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(1,-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-(x-1)+2(y+1) \\\\  & ≈2-x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938269ce-a097-533a-8835-0946dcc5ab22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124137} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc34039-753e-518d-bebb-608c0be2bf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124138} {:answer/id #uuid "e517fdad-8f01-5e47-89b0-0fb1d661c901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124139} {:answer/id #uuid "46cf9b3c-af1a-5e90-8b58-a91cddd43751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124140} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0775c0-16b3-5705-aeb6-e35bb2330d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124141}] :answer-field/id #uuid "89e18113-e03d-56c5-9c68-cd3d6dd4783f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124136}] :question/id #uuid "2f4dcdb5-6567-5f9f-b06c-b737ffa00be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97878" :question/problem "Let $f(x, y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n$f(1, 1) = 1, f_{x}(x, y) = 3x^{2}y^{4}, f_{y}(x, y) = 4x^{3}y^{3}$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1, 1)$, estimate the value of $f(1.02, 0.97)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ece6913-856c-52aa-b0d6-4c8f831172c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361181} {:answer/id #uuid "68d9d412-30dc-5773-bbe0-70e347695d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361309} {:answer/id #uuid "7402d50b-835f-5a97-b7be-235b0ebf8b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361347} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e42533-e885-5fa0-a753-04a0a85f22c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361627} {:answer/id #uuid "ec01897b-8f9c-5ad2-b9d6-7b57864a4503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1e42533-e885-5fa0-a753-04a0a85f22c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361627} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d463b53-79be-5ae4-ae8e-ddf38fcbe539" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "038d7eb3-5a24-5734-b4ea-86b820fa315c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98326" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,1)=2,\\,{f}_{x}(2,1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(2,1)=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.98,0.94).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,1)+∇f(2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,1),{f}_{y}(2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.98,0.94)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.98$ and $y=0.94,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.98,0.94) & ≈2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.98-2,0.94-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.02,-0.06⟩ \\\\  & =2+(-1)(-0.02)+(-1)(-0.06) \\\\  & =2+0.02+0.06 \\\\  & =2.08\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45bb1f2b-4770-5e9f-916d-76839911c3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385908} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd80317-f861-5477-b55a-d39cc46e39e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385909} {:answer/id #uuid "314ea318-63db-5fd0-ab48-1280dd3d2801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385910} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ae7c85-0f69-5ef6-a022-cca2272fc6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385911} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8cdb91-1d0b-540f-afd7-96af3aa4f6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd80317-f861-5477-b55a-d39cc46e39e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385909} :answer-field/id #uuid "311374b4-388a-5fad-bc7a-707753d226fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3f7ff9e-4535-5200-a21a-2208bf03983f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98333" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,2)=9,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=-\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{26-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{6y}{\\sqrt{26-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2),$ estimate the value of $f(3.05,2.05).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(3,2)+∇f(3,2)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,2)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,2),{f}_{y}(3,2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =9+⟨{f}_{x}(3,2),{f}_{y}(3,2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,2)$ and ${f}_{y}(3,2)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,2) & =-\\frac{3(3)}{\\sqrt{26-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}=-3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,2) & =-\\frac{6(2)}{\\sqrt{26-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈9+⟨-3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.05,2.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.05$ and $y=2.05,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.05,2.05) & ≈9+⟨-3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨3.05-3,2.05-2⟩ \\\\  & =9+⟨-3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.05,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =9-0.15-0.2 \\\\  & =8.65\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a22f39c-3e22-555a-9cbe-3427b089a85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360876} {:answer/id #uuid "269fe4c2-f6da-5679-85c2-111e189c3fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361003} {:answer/id #uuid "501247b0-48fd-5ca5-9d59-c73a57af252a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361190} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4909ee-9cba-55b3-8dba-c72429e003ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361402} {:answer/id #uuid "fde59fb9-f861-517e-a60f-a0c8c78437c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "501247b0-48fd-5ca5-9d59-c73a57af252a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361190} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa077fe-c758-5cca-956e-bfb0db4a1640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5611eac-4738-5020-8346-f839a127dd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-98342" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=-2,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(0.96,1.98,3.03).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.96,1.98,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.96,$ $y=1.98,$ and $z=3.03$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.96,1.98,3.03) & ≈3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.96-1,1.98-2,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,-0.02,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =3+0.08-0.02+0.09 \\\\  & =3.15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d1682a6-0223-5ed8-8277-0f5be1c651f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425837} {:answer/id #uuid "5c547bf0-6a15-5b93-836e-c0f81afbd41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425840} {:answer/id #uuid "c614f0d9-6fb7-53fc-b7ea-7df222acd263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425843} {:answer/id #uuid "ce98542a-f5c0-51c4-ab6c-242d4d9c6e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425845} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f80dc7-8dae-5243-9e68-a3715cc5329a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c614f0d9-6fb7-53fc-b7ea-7df222acd263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425843} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b6b4b5-7cfe-50c1-9f11-ac3785660947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e7afc5f-975b-58b4-8d28-e717181d277e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98349" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,3)=-9,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=x,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,3),$ estimate the value of $f(0.98,-0.01,3.1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,3)+∇f(1,0,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =-9+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,3),{f}_{y}(1,0,3),{f}_{z}(1,0,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,0,3)$, ${f}_{y}(1,0,3)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,0,3),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0,3) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0,3) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,0,3) & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.98,-0.01,3.1)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.98,y=-0.01$ and $z=3.1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.98,-0.01,3.1) & ≈-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨0.98-1,-0.01,3.1-3⟩ \\\\  & =-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.02,-0.01,0.1⟩ \\\\  & =-9-0.01-0.6 \\\\  & =-9.61\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9246113e-4d3c-5af3-89de-2a88d19d2249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈\\frac{x}{2}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361484} {:answer/id #uuid "d8dae7ed-ba7f-5011-a265-611f18d7540d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2x+\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361802} {:answer/id #uuid "f54d9284-4964-591c-b2c6-4b0c02314ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈16+2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361936} {:answer/id #uuid "f69ebb15-d980-5c11-9d90-52b205cd9816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈\\frac{x}{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361940} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f90fe0-2f06-51b6-b47c-1cea2e516996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈16+2x+\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8dae7ed-ba7f-5011-a265-611f18d7540d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2x+\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361802} :answer-field/id #uuid "e696106c-0a3d-5eed-98c0-3985a32cd8ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb8faba3-b294-554e-ae34-d852045d3d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-98551" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,8).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,8)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,8)+∇f(2,8)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,8)=8.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(2,8)=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{xy}} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(2,8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,8)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,8) & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,8)+∇\\,f(2,8)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-8⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-8⟩ \\\\  & =8+2(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-8) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2731dfc7-d2c6-5c2c-a042-6a0654dbb592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈35+9x+12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488553} {:answer/id #uuid "699fa767-12e0-5e62-9b8f-2b731f1a39e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈41+6x+12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488677} {:answer/id #uuid "7a449c97-159a-5f59-a721-89db59ab69e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈23-9x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488714} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fec697-0895-5a3c-96df-884cfd51b791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈35-9x+6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488806} {:answer/id #uuid "fb391dd4-bbf2-513b-9210-bd1b8336cab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈23+9x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2731dfc7-d2c6-5c2c-a042-6a0654dbb592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈35+9x+12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488553} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb4d8a0-1307-529a-b016-d7f1bd8d71e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a523061-b982-5542-a530-946bca7d0e33" :question/math-academy-id "q-98552" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-1.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,-3)+∇f(-2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x+2,y+3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-2,-3)=-19.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\, &  & ⇒\\,{f}_{x}(-2,-3)=(-3)^{2}=9, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =2xy &  & ⇒\\,{f}_{y}(-2,-3)=2(-2)(-3)=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-2,-3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,-3) & =⟨9,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,-3)+∇\\,f(-2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+3⟩ \\\\  & =-19+⟨9,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+3⟩ \\\\  & =-19+9(x+2)+12(y+3) \\\\  & =35+9x+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af3dd4b-8ac7-5c57-a6e6-d30e50a37bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404955} {:answer/id #uuid "be9eb651-901e-5a25-a042-03b339db014b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x+y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404956} {:answer/id #uuid "75095402-2c53-53be-8b08-c572a93a18b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404957} {:answer/id #uuid "de86e77b-b4ee-53ab-8132-4a467415d8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y+\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404958} {:answer/id #uuid "f010ac91-4556-5c1a-a8d5-df7a790c142d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5af3dd4b-8ac7-5c57-a6e6-d30e50a37bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404955} :answer-field/id #uuid "444731d2-c3ed-5654-abe5-5731357ba09e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0a65ec3-c24d-50c1-a1d2-f9666a246122" :question/math-academy-id "q-98554" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,1,e)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-\\frac{1}{z}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{z}(1,1,e)=-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,1,e)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1,e) & =⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+1(x-1)+1(y-1)-\\frac{1}{e}(z-e) \\\\  & =-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
